.\" printf.3 - formatted output conversion
.TH PRINTF 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
printf, fprintf, sprintf, snprintf, dprintf, asprintf,
vprintf, vfprintf, vsprintf, vsnprintf, vdprintf, vasprintf \-
formatted output conversion
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.br
.B #include <stdarg.h>
.PP
.BI "int printf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int fprintf(FILE *" stream ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int sprintf(char *" str ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int snprintf(char *" str ", size_t " size ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int dprintf(int " fd ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int asprintf(char **" strp ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int vprintf(const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vfprintf(FILE *" stream ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vsprintf(char *" str ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vsnprintf(char *" str ", size_t " size ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vdprintf(int " fd ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vasprintf(char **" strp ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR printf ()
family of functions produces output according to a
.IR format
as described below.
The functions
.BR printf ()
and
.BR vprintf ()
write output to
.IR stdout ,
the standard output stream;
.BR fprintf ()
and
.BR vfprintf ()
write output to the given output
.IR stream ;
.BR sprintf (),
.BR snprintf (),
.BR vsprintf (),
and
.BR vsnprintf ()
write to the character string
.IR str .
.PP
The functions
.BR dprintf ()
and
.BR vdprintf ()
write to the file descriptor
.I fd
directly using
.BR write (2),
bypassing any buffered I/O layer associated with that descriptor.
.PP
The functions
.BR asprintf ()
and
.BR vasprintf ()
allocate a string of sufficient size to hold the formatted output
(including the terminating NUL byte) using
.BR malloc (3),
and store a pointer to that string in
.IR *strp .
The caller is responsible for releasing the buffer with
.BR free (3).
.PP
The
.BR v
prefixed variants
.RB ( vprintf (),
.BR vfprintf (),
.BR vsprintf (),
.BR vsnprintf (),
.BR vdprintf (),
and
.BR vasprintf ())
are equivalent to the corresponding functions without the prefix, except
that they accept a
.I va_list
argument
.I ap
in place of a variable number of arguments. They do not invoke
.BR va_end (3)
on
.IR ap ;
the caller must do so.
.SS Format of the format string
The format string is composed of zero or more directives: ordinary
characters (not
.BR % ),
which are copied unchanged to the output stream; and conversion
specifications, each of which results in fetching zero or more subsequent
arguments. Each conversion specification is introduced by the character
.BR % ,
and ends with a
.IR "conversion specifier" .
In between there may be (in order) zero or more
.IR flags ,
an optional minimum
.IR "field width" ,
an optional
.IR precision ,
and an optional
.IR "length modifier" .
.SS Flag characters
The character
.B %
is followed by zero or more of the following flags:
.TP
.B \-
The converted value is left-adjusted on the field boundary.
(The default is right-justification.)
The converted value is padded on the right with blanks, rather than on the
left with blanks or zeros.
A
.B \-
overrides a
.B 0
if both are given.
.TP
.B +
A sign (+ or \-) shall always be placed before a number produced by a signed
conversion. By default a sign is used only for negative numbers.
A
.B +
overrides a space if both are used.
.TP
.B " "
(a space) A blank shall be left before a positive number (or empty string)
produced by a signed conversion.
.TP
.B #
The value is to be converted to an "alternate form".
For
.B o
conversions, the precision of the number is increased to force the first
character of the output string to be a zero (except when converting zero
with an explicit precision of zero, in which case nothing is produced).
For
.B x
and
.B X
conversions, a non-zero result has the string "0x" (or "0X") prepended.
.TP
.B 0
The value should be zero padded.
For
.BR d ", " i ", " o ", " u ", " x ", " X ", " e ", " E ", " f ", " F ", " g ", and " G
conversions, the converted value is padded on the left with zeros rather
than blanks.
If the
.B 0
and
.B \-
flags both appear, the
.B 0
flag is ignored.
For
.BR d ", " i ", " o ", " u ", " x ", and " X
conversions, if a precision is specified, the
.B 0
flag is ignored.
.SS Field width
An optional decimal digit string (with non-zero first digit) specifying a
minimum field width. If the converted value has fewer characters than the
field width, it is padded on the left (or right, if the left-adjustment
flag is given) to fill out the field width.
Instead of a decimal digit string the field width may be specified as
.BR * ,
in which case the value is taken from the next argument, which must be of
type
.BR int .
A negative field width is interpreted as a left-adjustment flag followed by
a positive field width.
.SS Precision
An optional precision, in the form of a period
.RB ( . )
followed by an optional decimal digit string. The precision may also be
specified as
.BR * ,
in which case it is taken from the next argument of type
.BR int .
If the precision is given as just
.B .
or as a negative value (via
.BR * ),
no precision is specified.
Precision gives the minimum number of digits to appear for
.BR d ", " i ", " o ", " u ", " x ", and " X
conversions; the number of digits to appear after the radix character for
.BR e ", " E ", " f ", and " F
conversions; the maximum number of significant digits for
.B g
and
.B G
conversions; or the maximum number of characters to be printed from a
string for
.B s
conversions.
.SS Length modifier
The optional length modifier specifies the type of the corresponding
argument.
.TP
.B hh
A following
.BR d ", " i ", " o ", " u ", " x ", or " X
conversion corresponds to a
.B "signed char"
or
.B "unsigned char"
argument (the argument will have been promoted to
.B int
according to the integer promotions, but its value is converted back before
formatting).
A following
.B n
conversion corresponds to a pointer to a
.B "signed char"
argument.
.TP
.B h
A following integer conversion corresponds to a
.B short
or
.B "unsigned short"
argument.
.TP
.B l
A following integer conversion corresponds to a
.B long
or
.B "unsigned long"
argument; for
.B s
the argument is treated as a pointer (length modifier is otherwise
ignored).
.TP
.B ll
A following integer conversion corresponds to a
.B "long long"
or
.B "unsigned long long"
argument.
.TP
.B j
A following integer conversion corresponds to an
.B intmax_t
or
.B uintmax_t
argument (currently treated identically to
.BR ll ).
.TP
.B z
A following integer conversion corresponds to a
.B size_t
or
.B ssize_t
argument.
.TP
.B t
A following integer conversion corresponds to a
.B ptrdiff_t
argument.
.TP
.B L
A following floating-point conversion corresponds to a
.B "long double"
argument (the value is narrowed to
.B double
before being formatted).
.SS Conversion specifiers
A character that specifies the type of conversion to be applied. The
conversion specifiers and their meanings are:
.TP
.BR d ", " i
The
.B int
argument is converted to signed decimal notation.
The precision, if any, gives the minimum number of digits that must
appear; if the converted value requires fewer digits, it is padded on the
left with zeros.
The default precision is 1.
When zero is printed with an explicit precision of 0, the output is empty.
.TP
.B u
The
.B "unsigned int"
argument is converted to unsigned decimal notation.
.TP
.B o
The
.B "unsigned int"
argument is converted to unsigned octal notation.
.TP
.BR x ", " X
The
.B "unsigned int"
argument is converted to unsigned hexadecimal notation.
The letters
.B abcdef
are used for
.B x
conversions; the letters
.B ABCDEF
are used for
.B X
conversions.
.TP
.BR f ", " F
The
.B double
argument is rounded and converted to decimal notation in the style
.RB [\-]ddd.ddd ,
where the number of digits after the decimal point is equal to the
precision specification.
If the precision is missing it is taken as 6; if the precision is
explicitly zero, no decimal point appears.
.TP
.BR e ", " E
The
.B double
argument is rounded and converted in the style
.RB [\-]d.ddd e \(+-dd
where there is one digit before the decimal point and the number of digits
after it equals the precision; if the precision is missing it is taken as
6.
The
.B E
conversion uses the letter
.B E
(rather than
.BR e )
to introduce the exponent.
The exponent always contains at least two digits.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
The
.B double
argument is converted in style
.B f
or
.BR e .
The precision specifies the number of significant digits.
If the precision is missing, 6 digits are given; if the precision is zero,
it is treated as 1.
Style
.B e
is used if the exponent from its conversion is less than \-4 or greater
than or equal to the precision.
Trailing zeros are removed from the fractional part of the result;
a decimal point appears only if it is followed by at least one digit, or if
the
.B #
flag is used.
.TP
.BR a ", " A
The
.B double
argument is converted to hexadecimal floating-point notation of the
form
.RB [\-]0x h . hhhh p \(+- d ,
where one hex digit precedes the radix character and the count of
hex digits following it is determined by the precision.
If the precision is missing, the precision is sufficient to
represent the value exactly (trailing zero nibbles are trimmed).
If the precision is zero and the
.B #
flag is absent, no radix character appears.
Rounding follows IEEE-754 round-to-nearest, ties-to-even.
The leading hex digit is
.B 1
for normal values and
.B 0
for subnormals and zero.
The binary exponent following
.B p
(or
.B P
for the
.B A
conversion) is the unbiased power of two as a signed decimal with at
least one digit.
.TP
.B c
The
.B int
argument is converted to an
.BR "unsigned char" ,
and the resulting character is written.
.TP
.B s
The
.B "const char *"
argument is taken to be a pointer to a string. Characters from the string
are written up to (but not including) the terminating NUL byte. If a
precision is specified, no more than that many bytes are written.
A NULL pointer is rendered as the literal text
.BR (null) .
.TP
.B p
The
.B "void *"
pointer argument is printed in lowercase hexadecimal preceded by
.BR 0x ,
zero-padded to at least eight hexadecimal digits.
.TP
.B n
The number of characters written so far is stored into the integer pointed
to by the corresponding argument. The
.BR l " and " ll
length modifiers select
.B "long *"
and
.B "long long *"
respectively; otherwise the argument is taken as
.BR "int *" .
No output is produced.
.TP
.B %
A literal
.B %
is written. No argument is converted. The complete conversion specification
is
.BR %% .
.PP
Unknown conversion specifiers are output as the literal
.B %
followed by the unknown character.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful completion, the
.BR printf (),
.BR fprintf (),
.BR sprintf (),
.BR dprintf (),
.BR vprintf (),
.BR vfprintf (),
.BR vsprintf (),
and
.BR vdprintf ()
functions return the number of characters transmitted (excluding the
terminating NUL byte for string outputs).
.PP
The
.BR snprintf ()
and
.BR vsnprintf ()
functions follow C99 semantics: they return the number of characters that
.I would
have been written had
.I size
been sufficiently large, excluding the terminating NUL byte.
Thus, a non-negative return value of
.I size
or more indicates that the output was truncated.
If
.I size
is greater than zero, the output is always NUL terminated.
If
.I size
is zero, nothing is written and
.I str
may be NULL; the would-have-written length is still returned.
.PP
The
.BR asprintf ()
and
.BR vasprintf ()
functions return the number of bytes that were allocated for the string
(excluding the terminating NUL byte) on success, and \-1 on allocation
failure; on failure
.I *strp
is left in an unspecified state and should not be used.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The
.BR asprintf ()
or
.BR vasprintf ()
function could not allocate sufficient memory for the result string.
The internal heap-fallback path used by
.BR printf (),
.BR fprintf (),
.BR vprintf (),
.BR vfprintf (),
.BR dprintf (),
and
.BR vdprintf ()
when the formatted output exceeds the internal 4096-byte stack buffer may
also fail to allocate memory; in that case the output is truncated to the
size of the internal buffer and the would-have-written length is still
returned.
.PP
The functions that write to a
.B FILE
stream or file descriptor may also fail with any of the errors specified
for
.BR fwrite (3)
or
.BR write (2).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates the C99 truncation semantics of
.BR snprintf ():
.PP
.nf
.RS
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char buf[8];
    int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "value=%d", 12345);
    /* n == 12, the length that *would* have been written. */
    /* buf contains "value=1\\0" - truncated, but NUL terminated. */
    printf("wrote %d bytes (would-be %d): \\"%s\\"\\n",
           (int)sizeof(buf) - 1, n, buf);
    if (n >= (int)sizeof(buf))
        printf("output was truncated\\n");
    return 0;
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
The following example uses
.BR asprintf ()
to format a string of unknown length without preallocating a buffer:
.PP
.nf
.RS
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *msg;
    int n = asprintf(&msg, "argv[0]=%s argc=%d", argv[0], argc);
    if (n < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "asprintf: out of memory\\n");
        return 1;
    }
    fputs(msg, stdout);
    fputc('\\n', stdout);
    free(msg);
    return 0;
}
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR write (2),
.BR fwrite (3),
.BR malloc (3),
.BR free (3),
.BR scanf (3),
.BR puts (3),
.BR setvbuf (3),
.BR stdarg (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR printf (),
.BR fprintf (),
.BR sprintf (),
.BR snprintf (),
.BR vprintf (),
.BR vfprintf (),
.BR vsprintf (),
and
.BR vsnprintf ()
functions conform to ISO C99.
.PP
The
.BR dprintf ()
and
.BR vdprintf ()
functions conform to POSIX.1\-2008.
.PP
The
.BR asprintf ()
and
.BR vasprintf ()
functions originated as GNU/BSD extensions and were standardized in
POSIX.1\-2024 (IEEE Std 1003.1\-2024).
.PP
The
.B a
and
.B A
conversion specifiers (hexadecimal floating-point) conform to ISO
C99.
